data.content:
Here's what changed and why:

**`TimeProvider`** (built into .NET 8+) is the lightest solution — no custom interfaces, no DI container needed.

```csharp
// Production — works exactly as before:
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator();

// Tests — fully controllable:
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, ...)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-5)); // can rewind/advance at will
```

For tests, add the NuGet package:
```
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

The optional parameter means **existing callers don't break** — `new InvoiceCalculator()` still uses real time. Apply the same pattern to any other class that reads `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`.
